# City research — worker instructions

## When this runs

- A new city is added, an existing city needs a refresh, or Nacho reports a
  QA correction (he's been there — that's Tier 4 QA).
- Research NEVER happens in the main build conversation. It always runs as
  parallel worker agents, one per city.

## The orchestrator prompt

When Nacho says "research these cities", spawn one worker agent per city (or
per small batch). All workers run in parallel and follow the per-city brief
below. Workers write two files and nothing else:

- `data/staging/cities/<slug>.json`
- `data/staging/reports/<slug>.json`

Never write `data/cities/`. Never write `apps/web/index.html`. Staging is the
inbox. It is empty when no city is in flight. Do not copy production cities
into staging.

## Per-city worker brief

1. **Verify, don't invent.** Every venue: name + address confirmed in ≥ 2
   independent tiers. DROP anything unverifiable — a thin file beats a
   fabricated one.
2. **Sources (every venue needs ≥ 2):** each source is
   `{tier, url, retrieved_at}`. Tiers: 0 = Overpass/OSM (addresses, coords),
   1 = Wikidata (stable facts), 2 = keyed APIs (only when keys exist),
   3 = MANUAL ONLY — the venue's own website, own Instagram, or own Facebook
   page (bio/highlights/posts via the checklist, never scraped). A Facebook
   page counts only when it is the venue's own page. Tier 4 is Nacho's
   QA — it is not a source.
3. **Hours — always check the venue's own website first.** The venue's own
   website is the #1 source for hours and the worker MUST fetch it (plain
   page read) and extract open/close times. `hours_verified: true` ONLY when
   the venue's own site, own Instagram, or own Facebook page states a full
   open AND close.
   Use `"no recent data"` ONLY when the venue's own site has no hours AND no
   other reliable source states them — never as a lazy default. The
   MANUAL_ONLY restriction applies to Instagram and Facebook (no public API,
   often login-walled: never log in, never scrape, use only what's publicly
   visible), NOT to venue websites, which workers always read.
   Whenever hours are verified, add the source they came from to the
   venue's `sources` array as an extra source (if not already listed) —
   every hours claim must be traceable to a cited tier-3 source.
   Verified format: `"24h"` or `{mon: ["10:00","02:00"], …}` in HH:MM.
4. **Coordinates:** numbers only when confidently sourced (map/geocode for
   the street address). Otherwise BOTH `lat` and `lon` are null. Never guessed.
5. **Categories.** The names anyone sees are Dance and drinks, Saunas,
   Cruisy, and Events. Events is not a venue category.
   It is a chip for dated events, shown only when the city has an event
   today or over the coming Friday–Sunday. Do not add Leather bars,
   Late-night clubs, Drag & cabaret, Drinks and Dance, Fun Fun, or any
   other taxonomy. A backroom stays in `review_features`, not a new group.
   The file still stores `category` as `bar` | `club` | `sauna` | `cruise` |
   `sex` so validation and the forecast keep working. That stored value is
   not a label. Map it: `bar` and `club` → Dance and drinks, `sauna` →
   Saunas, `cruise` and `sex` → Cruisy.
6. **Fields:** `id` is `<slug>-<name-slug>`, unique. `review_features`: 2–4
   short factual strings. `instagram`: handle or null. No unknown fields —
   the validator rejects them. No phone numbers, ever.
   `events_url` is optional: the venue's own events or what's-on page, or
   null. Omit it when there is no such page. It is a tier-3 source. Do not
   invent a calendar. `live_counter` is null, or the venue's own page when
   that page publishes a live headcount. Only Hotel Chilli in São Paulo has
   one: `https://hotelchilli.com.br/`. Do not invent a counter.
7. **Events:** `events` is an array on the city. v1 entries are prides only:
   `{name, type: "pride", start, end, source}`. Dates come from the
   organizer's published page, and `source` is that page. Use the full
   published range. If the organizer published one date, `start` and `end`
   are that date. Never infer a range from a headline day or from last
   year. Do not invent circuit, leather, or festival dates until a source
   is in hand. An empty array is honest.
8. **Meta:** `slug` (must match the filename), `name`, `country`,
   `region` (exact taxonomy string), `gayborhood` (string or null),
   city `lat`/`lon` (numbers), `status` (`seeded` for new cities),
   `last_updated` (YYYY-MM-DD), `tz` (IANA, from the reference table below).
9. **Minimum, then aim.** Fewer than 5 venues fails validation and must not
   merge. Aim for 6–8 covering Dance and drinks, Saunas, and Cruisy where
   they're genuinely part of the scene. Do not invent a venue to clear the
   floor. If a category truly has nothing verifiable, the report says so.
10. **Category audit.** For each stored category (`bar`, `club`, `sauna`,
    `cruise`, `sex`) run dedicated discovery queries, for example
    `gay sauna {city}`, `bathhouse {city}`, `{city} cruise bar`. Record every
    query, every page opened, and every candidate kept or dropped with a
    reason. "I searched and found nothing" is the queries plus the pages, not
    a sentence. The validator rejects a city whose report is missing.
11. **Anchor check.** Name the 2 or 3 venues a local guide would list first.
    Each is `included` (and present in the file) or `ruled_out` with a reason
    and a source url (closed, unverifiable, out of scope). This checks
    omission. It does not waive the two-source rule.

The report at `data/staging/reports/<slug>.json` is:

```json
{
  "slug": "london",
  "retrieved_at": "YYYY-MM-DD",
  "categories": [
    {
      "category": "sauna",
      "queries": ["gay sauna London", "bathhouse London"],
      "sources_checked": ["https://example.com/"],
      "candidates": [
        { "name": "Pleasuredrome", "kept": true, "reason": "name and address confirmed in two tiers" }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "anchors": [
    { "name": "Pleasuredrome", "decision": "included", "reason": "named by the venue's own site", "source": "https://example.com/" }
  ]
}
```

One audit object per stored category, each with at least one query and one
page opened. Candidates may be empty. Anchors are 2 or 3. A ruled-out anchor
needs `source`. No unknown fields.

## Validation and merge (in this order, no skipping)

1. `node scripts/select-venues.mjs` — validates every staging file against
   the schema, the 5-venue floor, and the research report. One city:
   `node scripts/select-venues.mjs tokyo.json`. Fix all failures. Review
   flags (thin category mix, single-tier confirmation, under half the venues
   with verified hours) are warnings printed before merge: investigate,
   don't invent. The hours flag means run the backfill pass before merge.
2. `node scripts/merge-cities.mjs` — copies only passing files to
   `data/cities/`, prints a diff per file, then deletes that city and its
   report from staging. Failing files stay in staging.
3. `node scripts/build-web.mjs` — rebuilds the page from `data/cities/`
   ONLY. Run it only when production holds exactly the cities you want
   published. The build checks the schema. It does not apply the 5-venue
   floor, so a city already published under that floor stays up until its
   worker replaces it.

## Learning loop (the rule that prevents repeat mistakes)

When Nacho spots a mistake, the fix goes INTO THIS BRIEF (or the spec),
then that city's worker re-runs under the stricter rule. Never patch a
venue by hand. Log every QA correction and what changed in the brief.

QA 2026-10-06 — Rome had zero saunas. A published city that has verifiable
bathhouses must include them. Do not stop at bars and clubs. For Rome,
verify and keep only saunas whose name and address are confirmed in two
tiers. Check Apollion Sauna (Via Mecenate 59/A), Adam Sauna (Via Pontremoli
28), and Europa Multiclub. Drop any that fail. Keep the venues already in
the live file. Stored category for a bathhouse is `sauna`.

QA 2026-10-06 — Steamworks Berkeley belongs in `san-francisco.json`. Nacho
goes there regularly: it is open, and an earlier closure claim was wrong.
Tier 4 is not a source. Still confirm the name and address in two tiers,
and take hours from the venue's own site. Do not drop it for that old
closure claim. Keep the other San Francisco venues.

QA 2026-10-06 — Berlin lists only Boiler Berlin as a sauna, which is too
thin. Cross-check several independent sources and add every bathhouse
whose name and address are confirmed in two tiers. Hours come from each
venue's own site. Drop anything you cannot verify. Keep the venues already
in the live file, including Boiler, unless a source shows it has closed.
Stored category is `sauna`. Tier 4 is not a source.

QA 2026-10-08 — Tokyo shipped with one venue and unverified hours. London
shipped with one sauna, and Pleasuredrome was missing. A city under 5 venues
now fails the merge gate. Every research run writes the category audit and
the anchor check. Re-run Tokyo. Re-run London's saunas, including
Pleasuredrome, and keep the venues already in the live file unless a source
shows one has closed. Do not invent a venue to clear the floor.

QA 2026-10-08 — Viña del Mar / Valparaíso. Nacho confirms Pagano and Club
Divino are open. Tier 4 is not a source. Keep Máscara, Templo Spa, and
Estación del Gato. Include Pagano (Avenida Errázuriz 1852, Valparaíso) and
Club Divino (Camino Internacional 537, Reñaca Alto, Viña del Mar). Do not
drop either because an earlier pass could not load a website, or because the
OSM building at that number was unnamed. Confirm each name and address in two
tiers, and take hours from the venue's own site, Instagram, or Facebook. If
only an open time is published, store that open time and keep
`hours_verified` false. Do not invent a close. Stored category is `club`.

QA 2026-10-08 — Santiago, Dionisio Divas. The card said Tuesday–Sunday from
23:00. The venue's own Facebook page (https://www.facebook.com/dionisiodivaspub)
says Wednesday–Sunday, open 21:00, show 23:00, humor, drinks, air-conditioned.
No close time is published. Store the open days as `["21:00"]` only
(Wednesday–Sunday), keep `hours_verified` false, and do not write
`"no recent data"` when the open time is published. The status line must use
that open time. Do not invent a close. A venue's own Facebook page is tier 3,
same manual rule as Instagram.

## Hours backfill pass

Run this when too many venues sit at `"no recent data"` for hours. It is a
separate operation from city research and can run while other work continues.

1. For every venue with `hours == "no recent data"`: fetch the venue's own
   website (plain page read — always allowed; MANUAL_ONLY is Instagram and
   Facebook only) and extract open/close times.
2. Full open AND close found → set `hours` (`"24h"` or
   `{mon: ["10:00","02:00"], …}` HH:MM), `hours_verified: true`, and add the
   site as a `{tier: 3, url, retrieved_at}` source if not already listed.
3. Genuinely no hours anywhere → leave `"no recent data"`, but only after
   exhausting all three: (a) the venue's own website thoroughly (homepage,
   contact/visit/hours pages — not a glance), (b) the venue's public
   Instagram or Facebook bio/highlights/posts, (c) the Google business listing. The
   report must say which of the three were checked per venue. Never invent
   hours, never touch other fields, keep ≥ 2 sources per venue.
4. Re-run `node scripts/select-venues.mjs` after every file; fix failures.
5. Write a short backfill report: per venue, hours found (with source URL)
   or still missing (with reason).

